	(text "7. Datasheet: https://www.nxp.com/docs/en/data-sheet/PCF8563.pdf"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 80.772 118.618 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(href "https://www.nxp.com/docs/en/data-sheet/PCF8563.pdf")
		)
		(uuid "b0d56aa7-a3d9-4747-ad5e-2241909cfe51")
	)
	(text "Notes to self:\n1. After power-on, the initial accuracy can be adjusted by testing the output frequency of CLKOUT and adjusting the matching capacitor value of the OSCI pin. If CLKOUT > 32.768kHz, the OSCI capacitor value needs to be increased;\nif CLKOUT < 32.768kHz, the capacitor value needs to be decreased.\n\n2. Generally, if using a 32.768kHz crystal oscillator with a load capacitor of 12.5pF, approximately a 25pF matching capacitor needs to be connected to the OSCI pin to adjust the initial accuracy.\n\n3. If noise or interference signals occur on the VCC power line, it is recommended to add resistors and capacitors to the power line for filtering.\n\n4. Crystal requirements from RTC datasheet: f = 32.768 kHz, CL = 12.5 pF, ESR = 50-100k\n\n5. Probably just shorting C2, but need to test note 2 so I kept the symbol and footprint there\n\n6. For the SAO, this is connected to 3v3. For the Swadge, this may go directly to VBAT. VDD is 1.0-5.5V, and the Lithium cell will only operate ~3.0V to 4.2V depending on our charge circuit settings.\nBecause this RTC may go straight to VBAT, we should consider protecting this chip with conformal coating or a physical cover to prevent shorting VBAT to GND. We should also consider wiring this after the charge\ncircuit or adding cutoff circuitry to prevent unsafe low voltage conditions, and determine a low voltage \"sleep\" state to reduce load on the battery and indicate that it needs charging, though it's never truly \"off\".\n\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 45.974 101.092 0)
		(effects
